 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N_{coarse} + N_{fine})$ network evaluations per ray [S1, S6]. The inverse CDF sampling adds $O(N_{coarse} \log N_{coarse})$ or $O(N_{coarse})$ depending on implementation, but is negligible compared to network inference [G].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N_{coarse} + N_{fine})$ network evaluations per ray [S1, S6].
 *   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N_{coarse} + N_{fine})$ per ray for storing sample positions, densities, and colors [G].
 *   **Measured Performance:** In NeRF-like settings, using $N_{coarse}=64$ and $N_{fine}=64-128$ provides a significant quality improvement over uniform sampling with similar total sample counts, or allows for higher quality with the same computational budget [S1, S6].
 *   **Strengths:** Efficiently allocates samples to complex regions, reducing noise and aliasing in detailed areas while maintaining speed in smooth regions [S4, unofficial][S6].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires two network passes (coarse and fine), increasing latency per ray. The quality of the fine sampling is dependent on the accuracy of the coarse network's density predictions [S6].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires two network passes (coarse and fine), increasing latency per ray, and the quality of the fine sampling is dependent on the accuracy of the coarse network's density predictions [S6].
 *   **When to Use:** When rendering scenes with high-frequency details or varying opacity, and computational resources allow for two network evaluations per ray [G].
 *   **When Not to Use:** In real-time applications with strict latency constraints where a single network pass is required, or in scenes with uniform density where uniform sampling is sufficient [G].
 
 ## Background
-Hierarchical sampling was introduced as a core component of Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) by Mildenhall et al. in 2020 [S3, unofficial][S6][S8, unofficial]. It was designed to address the issue that uniform sampling along rays is inefficient for scenes with complex geometry, as it wastes samples in empty space and under-samples detailed regions [S6][S8, unofficial]. The method builds on the idea of importance sampling, using a coarse estimate of the scene's density to guide the placement of finer samples [S1][S4, unofficial]. It has since become a standard technique in neural volume rendering and is used in many subsequent works, including [DDNeRF](8860-ddnerf.md) and [Hu et al. Density-based Sampling](7323-hu-et-al-density-based-sampling.md) [S1][S2, unofficial].
